  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Integrated response to drought affected community of Bugna woreda in Amhara Region of Ethiopia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ltpgtltspangtIn response to the severe drought affecting North Wollo Zone in Ethiopia&apos;s Amhara Region, the Emmanuel Development Association (EDA) proposes a multifaceted intervention to address the pressing needs of drought-stricken communities in Bugna Woreda. This 12-month project is aligned with the first standard allocation of the UN Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs&apos; (OCHA) Emergency Humanitarian Fund (EHF), aiming to provide critical support to communities in Bugna Woreda.lt/spangtlt/pgtltpgtThe proposal focuses on addressing the agricultural needs of drought-affected communities, enhancing the health and productivity of over 100,000 animals through livestock health services. Additionally, the project will provide agricultural inputs to 470 households, ensuring they have the necessary resources to maintain their livelihoods. Furthermore, to support&#xa0;emergency lifesaving MPC&#xa0;assistance will be provided to the most food insecure 760 households, helping to alleviate immediate hunger and malnutrition.ltbrgtThe project also includes training animal health workers, conducting animal health vaccination campaigns, establishing Animal Assistance Programs (AAP), and setting up beneficiary registration and selection committees. Moreover, the project will provide training on safe programming, PSEA (Protection from Sexual Exploitation and Abuse), and GBV (Gender-Based Violence) awareness, ensuring that all interventions are carried out in a safe and respectful manner.ltbrgtThis comprehensive approach aligns perfectly with the OCHA-EHF&apos;s goals, demonstrating EDA&apos;s commitment to providing a holistic response to the humanitarian crisis in Bugna Woreda. By addressing the critical needs of drought-affected communities, EDA aims to mitigate the devastating impacts of the drought and support the long-term resilience of these communities.lt/pgt</narrative>
  </description>
